  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 947 PM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA weak upper trough will slide SE through our area tonight, which\nmay produce enough lift to spark isolated showers along the LC-EP\ncorridor early in the morning. CAMs have backed off on this\nactivity in the last 24 hours, so chances are slim (10-20%). The\nlingering trough results in better E flow near the sfc Thu with\nisolated diurnal convection over area mtns during the afternoon. A\nshower or two cannot be ruled out for the lowlands. Otherwise, dry\nconditions with seasonal temps.\n\nUpper ridging builds in for Fri from the south, pushing the trough\nfurther east as it gets sucked into now Hurricane Isaias.\nSubsidence from the warmer air aloft should limit storm coverage\non Fri, even in the mtns.\n\nMoisture levels increase over the weekend as the ridge shifts east\nand troughing takes over the West Coast. Subtropical moisture is\npulled N ahead of an upper low/TS Rachel starting on Sat with\nmostly high level clouds. Rain chances are introduced for the Gila\non Sat, focusing out west through Sun. The combined upper low and\nremnants of Rachel will mainly impact the lower CO River Valley\nand AZ, bringing them the risk of heavy rainfall and flash \nflooding, including the Mogollon Rim. A few spotty showers are\npossible on Sun for much of our area with a low threat of flooding\nfor the Gila.\n\nThe rest of the period gets weird. TS Simon, which is expected to\nrapidly intensify, will track N through MX early next week with\nits moisture getting scooped up by the upper low/Rachel. It's\nuncertain the track and strength of Simon as it moves inland, but\nthe NHC track has the storm heading towards Sonora/Chihuahua. \nModels are struggling with rainfall amounts for this potential \nevent, but the latest NBM has everyone getting some rain in the\nmedian scenario, compared to basically nothing in the previous\nrun. Confidence is low in what our flooding risk will be Mon-\nTue, but we'll have our ears open to hear what Simon says\n(hopefully not a repeat of Polo).",
